    "---\n",
    "## 1. 1. Imperative Programming"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Brief Explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "In imperative programming you just write instructions one by one, in order. The computer follows them exactly as you wrote them. There are no functions, no classes — just variables, loops, and conditions. I think of it like giving someone directions step by step: \"first do this, then do that\"."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Code Example\n",
    "\n",
    "Complete the steps:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Given data\n",
    "grades = [70, 80, 90]\n",
    "total = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 1: Calculate sum using a loop\n",
    "for grade in grades:\n",
    "    total += grade\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 2: Compute average\n",
    "average = total / len(grades)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 3: Print result\n",
    "if average >= 70:\n",
    "    print(f'You pass with {average}')\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    print(f'You fail with {average}')"
   ]
