    "---\n",
    "## 1. 2. Structured Programming"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Brief Explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "Here you split the code into functions. Each function does one thing. Instead of writing the same steps over and over, you define them once in a function and then just call it. It makes the code shorter and easier to read. The control structures (if, for) are the same but now they live inside organized functions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Code Example\n",
    "\n",
    "Complete the steps:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Step 1: function to get the average\n",
    "def get_average(grades):\n",
    "    total = 0\n",
    "    for g in grades:\n",
    "        total += g\n",
    "    return total / len(grades)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 2: function to check if the student passes\n",
    "def check_status(average):\n",
    "    if average >= 70:\n",
    "        return \"PASS\"\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        return \"FAIL\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 3: call the functions\n",
    "grades = [70, 80, 90]\n",
    "avg = get_average(grades)\n",
    "result = check_status(avg)\n",
    "print(f\"Average: {avg:.1f} - {result}\")"
   ]
