    "---\n",
    "## 1. 4. Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Brief Explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "In OOP you create classes, which are like templates. From a class you can create objects, and each object has its own data and its own actions. For example, a Student object has a name, has grades, and knows how to calculate its own average. Everything about a student lives in one place instead of being scattered across different variables and functions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Code Example\n",
    "\n",
    "Complete the steps:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Step 1: create the Student class\n",
    "class Student:\n",
    "    def __init__(self, name, grades):\n",
    "        self.name = name\n",
    "        self.grades = grades\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Step 2: method to get the average\n",
    "    def get_average(self):\n",
    "        return sum(self.grades) / len(self.grades)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Step 3: method to check pass or fail\n",
    "    def get_status(self):\n",
    "        if self.get_average() >= 70:\n",
    "            return \"PASS\"\n",
    "        else:\n",
    "            return \"FAIL\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    def show(self):\n",
    "        print(f\"{self.name}: {self.get_average():.1f} --> {self.get_status()}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 4: create objects and use them\n",
    "s1 = Student(\"Ana\", [70, 80, 90])\n",
    "s2 = Student(\"Luis\", [50, 40, 60])\n",
    "s3 = Student(\"Maria\", [85, 90, 95])\n",
    "\n",
    "s1.show()\n",
    "s2.show()\n",
    "s3.show()"
   ]
  },
