    "---\n",
    "## 1. 5. Functional Programming (basic)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Brief Explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "In functional programming you use functions that don't change anything — they just receive data and return new data. Instead of loops you use map() and filter(). map() applies a function to every item in a list. filter() keeps only the items that match a condition. The idea is that nothing gets modified, you just transform the data step by step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Code Example\n",
    "\n",
    "Complete the steps:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# given data\n",
    "students = [\n",
    "    {\"name\": \"Ana\",   \"grades\": [70, 80, 90]},\n",
    "    {\"name\": \"Luis\",  \"grades\": [50, 40, 60]},\n",
    "    {\"name\": \"Maria\", \"grades\": [85, 90, 95]},\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 1: pure function to add the average to each student\n",
    "def add_average(student):\n",
    "    avg = sum(student[\"grades\"]) / len(student[\"grades\"])\n",
    "    return {\"name\": student[\"name\"], \"average\": avg}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 2: use map to apply the function to all students\n",
    "with_averages = list(map(add_average, students))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Step 3: use filter to get only the ones that pass\n",
    "passing = list(filter(lambda s: s[\"average\"] >= 70, with_averages))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"All students:\")\n",
    "for s in with_averages:\n",
    "    print(f\"  {s['name']}: {s['average']:.1f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nPassing:\")\n",
    "for s in passing:\n",
    "    print(f\"  {s['name']}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Your Modification\n",
    "\n",
    "**What did you change and why?**\n",
    "\n",
    "I added a second filter to get the students who fail, and then printed both groups separately. I also added the pass/fail label in the `add_average` function so each student already carries their status with them after the map step."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# my modification: added status in the function and split into two groups\n",
    "\n",
    "students = [\n",
    "    {\"name\": \"Ana\",   \"grades\": [70, 80, 90]},\n",
    "    {\"name\": \"Luis\",  \"grades\": [50, 40, 60]},\n",
    "    {\"name\": \"Maria\", \"grades\": [85, 90, 95]},\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "# pure function: takes a student, returns student with average and status\n",
    "def evaluate(student):\n",
    "    avg = sum(student[\"grades\"]) / len(student[\"grades\"])\n",
    "    status = \"PASS\" if avg >= 70 else \"FAIL\"\n",
    "    return {\"name\": student[\"name\"], \"average\": avg, \"status\": status}\n",
    "\n",
    "# map: apply evaluate to every student\n",
    "results = list(map(evaluate, students))\n",
    "\n",
    "# filter: split into two groups\n",
    "passing = list(filter(lambda s: s[\"status\"] == \"PASS\", results))\n",
    "failing = list(filter(lambda s: s[\"status\"] == \"FAIL\", results))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Passing students:\")\n",
    "for s in passing:\n",
    "    print(f\"  {s['name']}: {s['average']:.1f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nFailing students:\")\n",
    "for s in failing:\n",
    "    print(f\"  {s['name']}: {s['average']:.1f}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
