   "source": [
    "### Reflection\n",
    "\n",
    "**What did you learn from this approach and how does it compare to others?**\n",
    "\n",
    "This was the most different way of thinking for me. Instead of saying \"go through each student and do this\", you say \"apply this function to all of them\" and map does the work. filter was also easy to understand once I saw what it does — it's like a sieve that only lets certain items through. Compared to the other paradigms, there are no variables changing values, which I think makes it safer. The hardest part was getting used to not writing loops."
   ]
